<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Player, skill, camera, arena, growth and coffee. One file because tuning any of them means looking
  at the others: raising moveSpeed without checking the enemy table is how a build stops being playable.
-->
<Config>

  <!--
    maxSan 99 rather than 100. Every ceiling in this game is 99, and a player who notices it once
    starts looking for it everywhere.

    moveSpeed 4.5 is the number every enemy speed was chosen against. Raising it makes the whole
    enemy table trivially outrunnable, lowering it makes Deadline at 4.0 undodgeable. Treat it as
    fixed and tune the enemies instead.

    def 0 and dodge 0 at the start on purpose: the first armour piece has to feel like it did something,
    and a player who starts at 30 percent reduction cannot feel the difference of the next 10.
  -->
  <Player maxSan="99" atk="10" critChance="5" critMulti="200"
          def="0" dodge="0" dodgeCap="60"
          moveSpeed="4.5" haste="0" luck="0"
          invulnAfterHit="0.6"
          pickupRadius="1.6" stepPickupRadius="0.6"
          radius="0.4" />

  <!--
    摸鱼. Heals only 5 percent, so it is an escape button and not a heal button. The push exists to
    break a surround; without it the invulnerability just delays the same death by 1.5 seconds.
  -->
  <Skill id="slack" name="摸鱼" cd="12" invulnDuration="1.5" healPctMaxSan="5"
         pushRadius="3" pushForce="8" />

  <!--
    orthographicSize 5.5 at 16:9 gives a 19.6 x 11 view, so the camera half extents are 9.78 x 5.5.

    Those two numbers decide the spawn band, and the relationship is not the obvious one. An ellipse
    only encloses the screen rectangle when (halfW/semiX)^2 + (halfH/semiY)^2 <= 1: matching the axes
    to the screen leaves the ellipse cutting through all four corners, and enemies pop in diagonally.
    The band in Days.xml is sized against that inequality, so changing this line means resizing it too.
  -->
  <Camera orthographicSize="5.5" followLerp="6" aspect="1.7777778" />

  <!--
    Bounded field. A bounded arena is what stops "run left forever" from being the correct answer to
    pressure, and it is the precondition for the spawn band's edge fallback rule.

    Sized off the band, not off the screen: the band is anchored on the player, so the arena has to
    contain semi axis + outward push + edge margin or the top and bottom sectors fail their placement
    check on every attempt and the directional weighting quietly stops working.
  -->
  <!-- 19.71 / 11.5 matches the full 1834 x 1070 office map without cropping or stretching. -->
  <Arena halfWidth="19.71" halfHeight="11.5" />

  <!--
    ceil(coef * L^1.55) per level, 9 levels for the 9 job ranks.

    coef is 12 rather than the 10 in the design sheet, and the reason is the sheet's own stated intent:
    「玩家正好在周六升到 CEO」. At 10 the cumulative cost to rank 9 is 920 against an exp supply of about
    1230 across the six days, which seats the player as CEO two thirds of the way through Friday and
    leaves the entire last day without a single level up. At 12 the cost is 1103, landing on the
    Friday/Saturday boundary: promoted to CEO, then immediately the annual review. The rank curve is the
    anchor, not the coefficient, because it drives equipment card quality, the number of card choices
    and the result screen copy all at once.

    The first level up still costs 12, which is 6 mails or roughly 7 seconds. The hard requirement is
    that the first upgrade lands inside 10 seconds, and it depends on spawn density rather than on day
    length, so shortening Monday to 40 seconds does not threaten it.

    downgradeExp 3: a drop that cannot beat what is worn is still worth walking over. Without it the
    late game turns the floor into scenery, which quietly deletes the reason to move.

    kpiTargetKills 640 against the 672 bodies a clean full week produces, so the bar reaches the 99 cap
    partway through Saturday and clips. It has to clip somewhere: a target the run cannot reach leaves
    the bar at 98 and the cap never reads as a cap, while a much lower target parks it at 99 from
    Thursday onward and the cap stops reading as a cap and starts reading as a full bar. An average run
    that drops a third of the spawns lands in the high eighties, which is the intended near miss.
  -->
  <Progression maxLevel="9" expCoef="12" expPower="1.55" downgradeExp="3"
               kpiCap="99" kpiTargetKills="640" finalSalary="9996" />

  <!--
    The drop rate doubles below one third sanity. This is the one number in the game that lies to the
    player in their favour, and it is invisible by design: felt as a lucky break, never as a handout.
  -->
  <Coffee chancePct="4" lowSanChancePct="8" lowSanThresholdPct="33"
          healPctMaxSan="12" hasteAddPct="15" buffSeconds="4" viewId="v_coffee" />

</Config>
